#
# prima-materia — the searches of the Rasārṇava run, 2026-10-11
#
# Four searches over two chapters that were read complete, so each of them is a
# statement about the whole of paṭalas 1 and 18 and about nothing else. Two came
# back empty and one of those two is empty with a stated limit, because the
# chapter where the thing sought would stand is one of the fifteen that were not
# opened. Saying so is the point: a limit that stands in the record can be
# closed by whoever opens paṭala 10, and an unstated one cannot.
#
# In a file of its own for the mechanical reason recorded at
# examinations/searches-jain.ttl and in prima-materia#703.
#

pmp:RasarnavaTerminusDesistendiSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the Rasārṇava names a term after which a course that produced nothing is to be given up"@en, "Ob der Rasārṇava eine Frist nennt, nach der ein ergebnisloser Kurs aufzugeben ist"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Rasārṇava, paṭalas 1 and 18 complete, with 12.249-254, 2.13-18 and 2.126 (ed. Praphulla Chandra Ray and Harish Chandra Kaviratna, Bibliotheca Indica, Calcutta 1910), read in the Sanskrit."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage saying what the operator is to conclude, or to do, or to stop doing, when a stated term has passed and the promised result has not come: a number of attempts after which the preparation is wrong, a state of the body or of the substance that settles that this course will come to nothing, a term of abandonment as against the many terms of continuation the chapters give."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "Both chapters read at the wording, and the reading checked by term search of the Devanagari transcription for: न सिध्यति, न सिध्येत्, असिद्धि, निष्फल, विफल, मरण, म्रियते, मृत्यु, त्यजेत्, विवर्जयेत्, परीक्षा, लक्षण, दोष, अजीर्ण, दिन, मास, वर्ष, प्रहर, कल्प, and every verse-internal numeral of both chapters. Every hit read in its place."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:RasarnavaRasajirnalaksana ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-11" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-11" ;
    skos:note "One passage, and it is the strongest of its kind this holding has from this transmission. At 18.143 the work says that if in mercurial indigestion the mercury still does not digest, he dies, there is no doubt of it. That is a term of a sort: the course ends, and it ends in a fact. What the work does not supply anywhere is a term of abandonment in the ordinary sense, a point at which the operator who is neither dead nor accomplished should stop; the chapter has him continue."@en ;
    skos:note "Everything else the search returned is a term of continuation, and the distinction is the whole point. The chapters give three days four times over, fifteen nights, twenty-one days, one month, two months, three to ten months in a graded series, six months, one year, one year of the pill, three hundred years, sixteen times sixteen years, a thousand years, a myriad of Brahmā-days, the span of Brahmā, the span of Rudra, the span of a kalpa, and the dissolution of the world. Every one of them says how long to go on. Not one says when to stop."@en ;
    skos:note "And the work addresses the case all the same, five times, and every time in the operator or beyond him: for those lacking the discipline of the kula mercury does not succeed and they drink a mirage; for the unbeliever there is no accomplishment in ten billion births; without the mantra none in ten billion births; the work of one who has not served the guru is fruitless like a cloud at the dissolution of the world; and at the close of the operative part, the invisible thieves who carry the eaten mercury off and leave ash in the hand. Those are the nodes pmc:RasarnavaNastikasyaAsiddhi, pmc:RasarnavaMantraPurvakatva, pmc:RasarnavaGurvanujnaPurvakatva, pmc:RasarnavaVighnaharana and pmc:RasarnavaSastraDusanaAtmadosa. Together they are why no term of abandonment could stand in these chapters: a term would have to survive the sentence that the man who blames the treatise has not found the fault in himself, and it could not."@en .

pmp:RasarnavaDravyapariksaSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the two chapters state a way of knowing that the mercury is what it is called"@en, "Ob die beiden Kapitel eine Weise nennen, zu wissen, dass das Quecksilber das ist, wofür es gilt"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Rasārṇava, paṭalas 1 and 18 complete, with 12.249-254 (ed. Ray and Kaviratna, Bibliotheca Indica, Calcutta 1910), read in the Sanskrit. Explicitly not searched: paṭala 10, rasaśodhana, and paṭala 11, bālajāraṇa, which are unopened."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "An act with a definite perceptible outcome by which the operator establishes, before he eats it, that the substance in front of him is purified mercury, killed mercury, bound mercury or mercury that has digested the metal the recipe names: a test of the ash, of the globule, of the pill, of the degree of digestion."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "Both chapters read at the wording and checked by search for: परीक्षा, लक्षण, शुद्ध, अशुद्ध, जीर्ण, अजीर्ण, भस्म, चपल, निर्धूम, जानीयात्, विजानीयात्, ज्ञात्वा, दृष्ट्वा, भवति, तत्क्षणात्. Every hit read in its place."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-11" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-11" ;
    skos:note "Nothing of the kind in either chapter. What the chapters do carry are marks of a completed operation stated as the operation's own result and not as a test applied to it: the globule becomes a khoṭa that instant, the mercury becomes ash at six turns of the paste, the matter in the cauldron is to be worked until it is smokeless. Each of them tells the operator what will happen, not how to find out whether it did. The one near miss is the smokelessness of 18.216, which is a perceptible state and a condition for the next step, and it is a mark of the fire and not of the substance's identity."@en ;
    skos:note "The limit of this negative result is stated in the corpus line and is not a formality. The purification of mercury and the swallowing of the young mercury are paṭalas 10 and 11, and if the work has an assay of its own material that is where it stands. So this search establishes that the chapter which prescribes the eating and the chapter which announces the science both proceed without one, which is a weaker and a different finding from the work having none. A run that opens paṭala 10 should come back to this node."@en ;
    skos:note "Why the absence in paṭala 18 is worth recording anyway. That chapter has the operator eat a substance whose wrong state it says will kill him, inside a regimen of some forty observances whose transgression it says to mark by seven and twenty bodily signs. It is therefore the place in the work where an assay of the material would do the most work, and the chapter puts the whole burden of the diagnosis after the eating instead of before it."@en .

pmp:RasarnavaSiddhilaksanaSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the two chapters state signs of success, and how many against the conditions of failure"@en, "Ob die beiden Kapitel Gelingenszeichen nennen, und wie viele gegen die Misslingensbedingungen"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Rasārṇava, paṭalas 1 and 18 complete (ed. Ray and Kaviratna, Bibliotheca Indica, Calcutta 1910), read in the Sanskrit."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "Anything the chapters name as a sign that the operation has worked — a light, a warmth, an apparition, a change in the body — counted separately from the conditions of failure, as SPEC §15 requires, so that a transmission with many announced signs and no stated failure is recognisable as such."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "Both chapters read at the wording and checked by search for: लक्षण, प्रत्यय, चिह्न, आभ, प्रभा, तेज, वर्ण, नील, अरुण, संकाश, दृश्य, अदृश्य, उत्तिष्ठति, रव, घण्टा, and the colour words of 18.158. Every hit read in its place."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:RasarnavaDehavedhatKhecarata ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-11" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-11" ;
    skos:note "The count, which is the result. Signs of success in the two chapters: four, and they are of two grades. The weak grade is the colour of the operator's own body changing with the metal used — blue-tinged with steel, red-glowing with copper, moon-like with silver, gold-hued with gold, at 18.158 — which is announced in advance and is a matter of report. The strong grade is the transmutation of clay, stone and metal by the operator's urine, faeces, sweat and touch, at 12.251, 18.28-29 and 18.162-163, which is an assay. Beside them stand the great roar and the bell-sound of the cauldron rite at 18.216 and 18.222, which are signs of a performance nobody has reported performing. Conditions of failure, counted separately: one, the sign-set of mercurial indigestion at 18.137-143, with death as its outcome."@en ;
    skos:note "Four signs against one condition is a better ratio than most of this holding's opened works, and the one condition is unusually good. That is the finding and it goes against the shape of the corpus so far, which prima-materia#640 records as seven opened works, twelve announced signs and no condition of failure at all. This work supplies the missing side, and it supplies it in the same chapter as the most elaborate immunisation in the holding, which is why neither half of it should be reported alone."@en ;
    skos:note "What the strong grade is worth, stated once here and not repeated at the nodes. A sign announced to the practitioner and reported by him has an unknown base rate, which is why this holding counts signs apart from failures. The transmutation claims are not of that kind: the thing that changes is not the practitioner and not his perception, and an assay by somebody who thinks the whole doctrine false comes out the same way if the claim is true. That makes them the examinable material of this work and the reason it was worth the run."@en .

pmp:RasarnavaStriyogaSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the two chapters state that a second person is needed, and what is said of her"@en, "Ob die beiden Kapitel eine zweite Person verlangen, und was von ihr gesagt wird"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Rasārṇava, paṭalas 1 and 18 complete, with 2.13-18 (ed. Ray and Kaviratna, Bibliotheca Indica, Calcutta 1910), read in the Sanskrit."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A statement that the operation requires a participant other than the operator, and what the text makes the requirement turn on: a bodily mark, a calendrical state, a disposition, a consent. The question is put because this holding carries a statement about the second participant who does not enter the state, and because a requirement of this kind decides whether a performance could be carried out by one person at all."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "Read at the wording and checked by search for: नारी, स्त्री, युवती, कामिनी, कुमारी, शक्ति, देवी, मैथुन, आलिङ्गन, स्पर्शन, ऋतुमती, पुष्पदर्शन, कृष्णपक्ष, शुक्लपक्ष, एकचित्ता. Every hit read in its place."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:RasarnavaSastragniAvadhyata ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-11" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-11" ;
    skos:note "Found, and the requirement is stated twice in paṭala 18 in identical verses and once more in paṭala 2. The woman is specified by marks: hair curling at the tips, dark-complexioned, lotus-eyed, broad of hip, with a tapering breast, and showing her courses in the dark fortnight; such a one is declared a kāminī and the best in the elixir. The chapter then puts the effect on the operator — in embracing, in touching, in intercourse and in conversation he is freed of all disease and without greying or wrinkle — and adds that a black or pale or ill-conducted woman is never to be resorted to, and that she is to be kept close and well guarded. Paṭala 2 states the same kind of requirement in the calendrical form, classing women as kākiṇī, kokaṇī and kāñcikācinī by the fortnight in which their courses fall, and listing the woman who is to be avoided, among them the one who reviles mercury."@en ;
    skos:note "What the search establishes for the holding's statement about the one who stays outside. The Rasārṇava's second participant is required, specified and not said to undergo anything: every effect named in the passage falls on the operator, and nothing is claimed of her except the marks that qualify her. Her consent is not mentioned, and the one thing said of her state is that she is to be guarded. So this is a case of the pattern at its sharpest, and it is entered inside the claim nodes rather than as a capacity of its own because the work never ascribes her a capacity."@en ;
    skos:note "Why the marks matter beyond the ethnography. Every one of them is a visible or calendrical fact about a third person, so the work makes its own precondition checkable in a way it never makes the outcome: whether a woman's courses fell in the dark fortnight is a question with an answer. The chapter therefore has at its disposal exactly the kind of statement it does not make about the result."@en .
